 cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 15 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:AMaPO: Adaptive Margin-attached Preference Optimization for Language Model Alignment\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ruibo Deng , cite7†Duanyu Feng , cite8†Wenqiang Lei L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled AMaPO: Adaptive Margin-attached Preference Optimization for Language Model Alignment, by Ruibo Deng and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Offline preference optimization offers a simpler and more stable alternative to RLHF for aligning language models. However, their effectiveness is critically dependent on ranking accuracy, a metric where further gains are highly impactful.\nL17: This limitation arises from a fundamental problem that we identify and formalize as the Overfitting-Underfitting Dilemma: current margin designs cause models to apply excessive, wasteful gradients to correctly ranked samples (overfitting) while providing insufficient corrective signals for misranked ones (underfitting). To resolve this dilemma, we propose Adaptive Margin-attached Preference Optimization (AMaPO), a simple yet principled algorithm.\nL18: AMaPO employs an instance-wise adaptive margin, refined by Z-normalization and exponential scaling, which dynamically reallocates learning effort by amplifying gradients for misranked samples and suppressing them for correct ones. Extensive experiments on widely used benchmarks demonstrate that AMaPO not only achieves better ranking accuracy and superior downstream alignment performance, but targeted analysis also confirms that it successfully mitigates the core overfitting and underfitting issues.\nL19: Comments:  | AAAI 2026 AIA oral, our code is available at cite11†this https URL†github.com L20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.09385 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.09385v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09385†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Ruibo Deng [cite15†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 14:51:59 UTC (133 KB)\nL28: [v2] Sat, 15 Nov 2025 04:40:53 UTC (134 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled AMaPO: Adaptive Margin-attached Preference Optimization for Language Model Alignment, by Ruibo Deng and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmap